.TH TULPAR-JSON 7 "2026" "tulpar 0.7.0" "Tulpar Manual"
.SH NAME
tulpar-json \- machine-readable output of tulpar
.SH DESCRIPTION
Commands that accept
.B \-\-json
print a single JSON document on standard output, terminated by a newline.
Diagnostics, warnings and prompts go to standard error, so the output can be
piped straight into a JSON parser. The exit status is the same as without
the option.
.PP
The schemas below are covered by the unit tests. New fields may be added in
later versions; existing fields are not removed or changed in type, so
consumers should ignore keys they do not know. Strings are UTF-8. Optional
fields are marked as such and are omitted rather than set to
.BR null ,
except where stated.
.SH SCHEMAS
.SS list
An array of objects, one per installed package:
.TP
.B name, version, description
Strings. The description is empty if the package has none.
.TP
.B installed_by_hand
Boolean, true if the package was installed explicitly.
.TP
.B held
Boolean.
.TP
.B available_version
Optional string. Present only with
.B \-\-upgradable
and holds the newer version found in the repositories.
.SS info
One object:
.TP
.B name, version, type, architecture, maintainer, license, homepage, description
Strings, empty when unknown.
.TP
.B dependencies, provides, conflicts, replaces, required_by
Optional arrays of strings, omitted when empty. Dependencies keep their
version constraints, for example
.BR "libfoo >= 1.2" .
.B required_by
is present only for installed packages.
.SS search
An array of objects with the strings
.BR source ", " name ", " version " and " description .
.B source
is either
.B local
or
.BR remote .
.SS history
An array of objects, oldest first:
.TP
.B op
One of
.BR install ", " remove " or " rollback .
.TP
.B name, version
Strings.
.TP
.B status
.B ok
or
.BR failed .
.TP
.B timestamp
Unsigned integer, seconds since the Unix epoch.
.TP
.B explicit
Boolean, true if the operation was requested directly rather than pulled in
as a dependency.
.SS owns
An array with one object per requested path, in the order given:
.TP
.B path
String, the path that was looked up, made absolute.
.TP
.B owner
The owning package name, or
.B null
if no package owns the path. This is the only place where
.B null
is used.
.SS files
One object with the strings
.B name
and
.BR version ,
and the array of strings
.BR files ,
the absolute paths recorded for the package.
.SS why
One object:
.TP
.B name
String.
.TP
.B explicit
Boolean, true if the package was installed explicitly.
.TP
.B required_by
Array of the installed packages that depend on it.
.TP
.B chain
Array of package names leading from an explicitly installed package to this
one, ending with
.BR name .
Empty for explicit packages and for orphans.
.TP
.B orphan
Boolean, true if the package is a dependency that nothing requires or that
only unused packages require, the same set as
.BR tulpar-orphans (1)
reports.
.SS stats
One object with the integers
.BR packages ", " explicit ", " dependencies ", " held ", " orphans " and " files .
.SS repo list
An array of objects in consultation order:
.TP
.B url
String.
.TP
.B enabled
Boolean.
.TP
.B priority
Integer, lower is consulted first.
.TP
.B scope
.B system
or
.BR user ,
the list file the entry comes from. Disabled entries are always included.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tulpar (1),
.BR tulpar-list (1),
.BR tulpar-info (1),
.BR tulpar-history (1),
.BR tulpar-repo (1)
